IBM reports a global pharmaceutical manufacturer facing:
- inconsistent risk classification;
- manual and time-intensive risk processes;
- limited visibility into alternative suppliers and materials;
- inefficient analysis requiring numerous reports;
- slow decision cycles.
Source:
https://www.ibm.com/case-studies/global-pharmaceutical-manufacturer
The solution included:
- impacted-supplier drilldowns;
- demand and supply analysis;
- alternate sites or materials;
- batch tracking;
- bill-of-material mapping;
- multidimensional risk analysis.
Source:
https://www.ibm.com/case-studies/global-pharmaceutical-manufacturer

IBM reports using AI to monitor external disruption signals and act quickly to secure a second supply source.
Source:
https://www.ibm.com/case-studies/ibm-supply-chain
The case describes:
- demand sensing;
- external disruption monitoring;
- risk management;
- rapid action to secure a second source;
- integrated information for employees to understand and mitigate disruptions.

Deloitte describes a global automotive OEM that historically relied on reactive and decentralized supplier-risk measures.
The organization had limited visibility into commodities and suppliers.
Deloitte developed:
- key risk dimensions;
- internal and external data sources;
- an analytical risk model;
- prioritized commodity-supplier combinations;
- visualization and drill-down capabilities;
- a risk mitigation playbook.
The program prioritized more than 6,000 commodity-supplier combinations into an actionable subset of high-risk pairs.
Source:
https://www2.deloitte.com/us/en/pages/operations/solutions/supplier-risk-management.html

Accenture describes a supply-chain resilience program using:
- supplier/component risk heat maps;
- a supply-chain digital twin;
- disruption stress testing;
- resiliency scoring;
- mitigation analysis.
The program reportedly reduced revenue at risk by several hundred million dollars.
Source:
https://www.accenture.com/us-en/case-studies/supply-chain-operations/risk-management-supply-chain-resilience

The case states that the organization faced risks including:
- discontinuity of supply;
- potential manufacturing-facility shutdown;
- expedited-shipping costs;
- inefficient planning;
- poor supplier communication;
- production delays.
Source:
https://documents.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/BPD/Flyers/case-study-cgmp-chemicals-and-sourcing-services-alternative-sourcing.pdf

The documented response included:
- identifying alternative suppliers;
- using market intelligence;
- auditing and qualifying the new supplier;
- establishing a management-of-change process;
- accelerating material delivery;
- establishing shipping lanes and freight arrangements.
Source:
https://documents.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/BPD/Flyers/case-study-cgmp-chemicals-and-sourcing-services-alternative-sourcing.pdf

The alternate sourcing strategy helped the organization:
- avoid production delays and shutdowns;
- obtain production-critical chemical supply;
- establish an alternate source;
- improve operational efficiency.
Source:
https://documents.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/BPD/Flyers/case-study-cgmp-chemicals-and-sourcing-services-alternative-sourcing.pdf

The Ford case considers a just-in-time environment with:
- a small supplier base;
- low inventory levels;
- critical parts;
- a primary and potential secondary supplier.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2021.1975058

The model explicitly considers disruption of the primary supplier and the resulting uncertainty about:
- disruption probability;
- time to recover;
- secondary-source availability;
- response time.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2021.1975058

The Ford study evaluates multiple mitigation strategies, including:
1. reserve backup capacity from the primary supplier;
2. reserve capacity from a secondary supplier;
3. pre-qualify a secondary supplier;
4. hold backup inventory;
5. use different dual-sourcing configurations.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2021.1975058

A cross-functional task force was formed involving:
- R&D;
- management;
- communication;
- manufacturing;
- regulatory support;
- customer support;
- regional representatives;
- supply-chain experts.
The organization also immediately evaluated and qualified a new supplier.
Source:
https://www.bioprocessintl.com/biochemicals-raw-materials/supply-chain-challenges-in-the-biopharmaceutical-industry-a-case-study-following-the-2011-tsunami-in-japan

