https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/source-to-pay-operations/sourcing-and-procurement-operations/spo-spend-mgmt.html

The Ford study uses a multistage stochastic programming framework.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2021.1975058
The ServiceNow documentation reviewed does not establish an equivalent native stochastic optimization engine for:
- disruption probability;
- recovery-time uncertainty;
- backup capacity;
- backup inventory;
- secondary-source combinations;
- expected disruption cost;
- service/backorder consequences.

Supplier-disruption strategy selection is a real decision class.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2021.1975058
However, the research does not establish:
- frequency for ServiceNow customers;
- decision volume;
- current manual effort;
- economic cost of the decision process;
- failure rate;
- time-to-decision;
- executive escalation burden.

No source reviewed establishes:
- customer willingness to pay;
- budget allocation;
- procurement demand for an additional decision layer;
- dissatisfaction with ServiceNow;
- demand for a specialized supplier-resilience intelligence product.

Peer-reviewed research demonstrates that supply-chain disruption recovery can involve changing conditions across time.
One multi-stage disruption model considers:
- disruption types;
- recovery time;
- alternative suppliers;
- production quantities;
- raw-material inventory;
- customer orders;
- product-change decisions;
- backorder costs;
- lost sales.
Source:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8062424/

