inferences.statement:
There is no evidence that an optimizer-to-ServiceNow handoff necessarily requires a new intelligence layer.
A mature integration can pass:
- supplier state;
- inventory state;
- order state;
- availability;
- shipment information;
- revised recommendations;
- execution requirements.
ServiceNow documents inbound procurement data integrations including:
- availability updates;
- order acknowledgements;
- shipment updates;
- supplier-product data.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/source-to-pay-operations/sourcing-and-procurement-operations/spendint-api.html

hypotheses.statement:
Only evidence showing a recurring boundary such as:
STRATEGIC OPTIMIZER
↓
PRODUCES GENERIC / STATIC RECOMMENDATION
↓
REAL-WORLD CONTEXT CHANGES
↓
BUSINESS RULES / POLICY / HUMAN CONSTRAINTS
↓
RECOMMENDATION MUST BE RE-EVALUATED
↓
SERVICE NOW WORKFLOW
↓
HUMAN RECONSTRUCTION
For example:
- optimizer says "use supplier B";
- supplier B has become unavailable;
- customer priority changed;
- regulatory constraint changed;
- production priority changed;
- contract constraint changed;
- recovery estimate changed;
- another supplier became available;
- the recommendation must be recomputed in context.
That could create a genuine **decision-continuity gap** between static optimization and operational execution.

